course: dictionary
lesson: 5179
title: Italian: one word, three jobs
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can do different jobs in a sentence. Italian is a good example. As an adjective it describes a thing: «Italian food» means food from Italy. As a noun it names a person or a language: «An Italian» is a person from Italy, and Italian is also the language they speak.
explain: Look at the words around it to see which job it is doing. «She wants to speak Italian on holiday.» Here Italian is the language, because you speak a language. «He ordered an Italian meal» describes the meal, so Italian is describing.
explain: Notice the capital letter. Italian starts with a capital I because it names a country, a people or a language. Ask yourself: is the word describing something, or naming someone or something? That tells you its job.
fact: Italian with a capital I means from or about Italy, a country in southern Europe.
fact: An Italian is a person from Italy. The plural is Italians.
fact: Italian is also the language spoken in Italy and parts of Switzerland.
fact: Switzerland has four national languages: German, French, Italian and Romansh.
fact: Italian grew from Latin, like Spanish and French, so these languages are cousins.
exercise: «Giulia cooked an Italian meal for her friends.» What kind of word is Italian here? | A noun, because it names a person from Italy | *An adjective, because it describes the meal | A verb, because it shows an action | A noun, because it names a language
exercise: «Switzerland has four national languages, German, French, Italian and Romansh.» In this sentence, Italian is a | A country | *A language | A person | An action
exercise: Why does Italian start with a capital I? | Because it is the first word of a sentence | Because it is a question | Because it is a long word | *Because it names a country, a people or a language
test: Which word in «An Italian lives in Rome» names a person? | In | *Italian | Lives | Rome
test: Why is Italian an adjective in «She loves Italian food»? | It names a language | *It describes the food | It names a person | It starts the sentence
exam: Which sentence uses Italian as the language? | «Many Italians live in Canada.» | *«She can speak Italian.» | «He ate an Italian ice cream.» | «The Italian flag is green, white and red.»
